￼
Implicit specification assumes two premises. 

First, LLMs can detect subtle cues in implicit language. Second, once detected, LLMs can role-play the specification.

CoBRA does not assume those premises.

We treat them as empirical questions that require study and validation.


Page 6:
￼
We use the framing-effect experiment as our pilot study to understand whether these premises hold or not. 

In the classic human experiment setup, participants read a scenario—for example, a disease outbreak is expected to kill many people. Two programs that describe the same outcome are proposed.  One is phrased as a positive frame. The other is phrased as a negative frame.

Although they are equivalent, common people tend to favor the positive frame and choose program A. This is called the framing effect.

Studies’ results show that the domain expert, for example, an economist, typically shows less framing effect than the common person, because they have relevant background knowledge and have received professional training.

We use this validated experiment to test whether implicit specification can capture these behavioral differences and maintain consistent behavior when models are changed.


Page 7:
￼

We ran 3 agent profiles across 4 models: common refers to a pharmacy shopkeeper persona, and economist refers to a professor of economics. We only vary the occupation background while keeping other descriptions the same.

Page 8:
￼

Our results show that

The same specification produced inconsistent framing behavior across models

The implicit specification did not reliably produce the expected behavior. 
For instance, “economist” did not consistently behave less framing-sensitive than the layperson, which contradicts the human results.

These findings suggest the premises behind implicit agent specification are not reliable, which highlights the need for explicit, quantitative control that we can verify.

